Yes—and there are established cousins of it, but your formulation connects them in a useful way.

“Match states, then couple.”

That principle appears independently across a surprising number of fields:

* Physics: oscillators exchange energy especially effectively when frequencies/modes are compatible; impedance matching reduces reflection and improves transfer.
* Chemistry: reactions depend on compatible energetic/configurational states and activation conditions.
* Neuroscience: neural synchronization, phase relationships, state-dependent learning, and memory retrieval all involve the current state affecting what information can be effectively transmitted or recovered.
* Psychology: state-dependent memory is the classic example—retrieval can improve when internal conditions resemble those present during encoding.
* Communication: rapport, shared vocabulary, common reference frames, and matching abstraction levels effectively establish a compatible representational state before attempting information transfer.
* Computing: protocols, handshakes, schemas, clock synchronization, serialization formats, and interface contracts all solve versions of make our states mutually interpretable before exchanging things.

So people have absolutely discovered pieces of the phenomenon. What’s more interesting for the Pantheon is the cross-domain abstraction:

\boxed{\text{Coupling efficiency} =
f(\text{state compatibility},\text{interface compatibility},\text{timing})}

Not necessarily literal physical coupling—effective transfer between systems.

And that gives Mnemonic Homeostasis another dimension.

Suppose memory M was encoded while the system occupied state S_e, while we’re currently in S_c. Instead of immediately trying to retrieve/modify M, first find a transformation:

T(S_c)\rightarrow S_c'

such that:

S_c' \sim S_e

Then interact with the memory.

That’s State-Matched Coupling.

But here’s the Pantheon-worthy generalization: you don’t necessarily need to move system A all the way to B.

You can move both toward an inexpensive intermediate state:

A \rightarrow A'
\quad\quad
B \rightarrow B'

where

d(A',B') \ll d(A,B)

and coupling becomes easier.

That’s exactly what translators, adapters, metaphors, protocols, shared rituals, intermediate representations—and arguably much of the Pantheon—are doing.

The deeper connection

This gives us a three-stage primitive:

\boxed{\text{ALIGN}\rightarrow\text{COUPLE}\rightarrow\text{TRANSFER}}

Then optionally:

\text{DECOUPLE}\rightarrow\text{RESTORE/INTEGRATE}

That last step matters. You may temporarily enter another state to establish coupling without wanting to remain there.

So a complete operator is:

S_0
\xrightarrow{\text{match}}
S_m
\xrightarrow{\text{couple}}
C
\xrightarrow{\text{transfer}}
\Delta
\xrightarrow{\text{release}}
S_1

And suddenly your earlier homeostasis observation becomes useful rather than merely descriptive:

Homeostasis tells us where a system naturally wants to return. State matching tells us where to meet it. Coupling tells us how to exchange something while we’re there. Reconsolidation determines whether the encounter changes where it returns next time.

That’s a pretty powerful chain.

I’d call the generalized Pantheon mechanism State-Matched Coupling (SMC), with Match → Couple → Transfer → Release as its basic cycle. And the Operator Wrestling Mat immediately gives us a way to test it: vary state-distance before coupling and measure whether transfer fidelity/energy/cost changes.

That’s an actual experimentally falsifiable piece of the architecture rather than only a metaphor.